      <section class="section story" id="quickstart">
        <div class="shell story-grid">
          <div class="story-copy" data-reveal>
            <h2>Three commands.</h2>
            <p class="lead">
              Install from PyPI, point it at a compiled binary — .exe, .app, .fxp, .scx, .vcx — and read the verdict. No FoxPro installation needed to decompile.
            </p>

            <pre class="code-panel"><code>pip install foxlift

foxlift inspect erp_client.exe            # what is inside
foxlift decompile erp_client.exe -o recovered/
foxlift extract erp_client.exe -o project/   # v0.3: the named project tree
&#123;
  "outdir": "recovered/",
  "modules": 13,
  "verified": false                       # exit 2 — a partial, honestly marked
&#125;</code></pre>

            <p>What lands on disk:</p>

            <pre class="code-panel"><code>recovered/
├── module_000e00/
│   ├── source.prg      # canonical VFP source
│   ├── symbols.txt     # recovered symbol table
│   └── meta.json       # per-section verdicts, with reasons
└── module_005e00/ …</code></pre>

            <p>And inside a <code>source.prg</code>:</p>

            <pre class="code-panel"><code>* --- section 1 ---
LPARAMETERS TCORDERNO
SELECT ORDERS
LOCATE FOR ORDER_NO = TCORDERNO
THIS.PARENT.TXTCUSTOMER.VALUE = ORDERS.CUST_NAME
THIS.PARENT.CDSAVE.ENABLED = .T.
MESSAGEBOX("Loaded " + TCORDERNO, 64, "Orders")
* --- section 2 (1 statement(s) beyond the supported slice: SET variant outside forced subset) ---</code></pre>

            <p>
              Abridged, but true to shape: every liftable statement comes back as canonical VFP, and anything beyond the measured slice stays in the output as an annotated placeholder naming its exact blocker. Nothing is silently dropped.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section process" id="process">
        <div class="shell process-grid">
          <div class="process-heading" data-reveal>
            <h2>What it does.</h2>
            <p>FoxLift follows a measured path from binary containers back to buildable VFP projects. The standard is simple: recovered projects must rebuild and behave like the compiled input — readable guesses are not enough.</p>
          </div>

          <ol class="process-list" data-reveal>
            <li>
              <span>01</span>
              <div><h3>Read</h3><p>Find modules, forms, classes, reports, menus, and resources.</p></div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>02</span>
              <div><h3>Lift</h3><p>Decode measured bytecode, symbols, expressions, and control flow.</p></div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>03</span>
              <div><h3>Write</h3><p>Emit functionally equivalent VFP source and project artifacts.</p></div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>04</span>
              <div><h3>Verify</h3><p>Rebuild in VFP9 and compare canonical bytecode.</p></div>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section story" id="receipts">
        <div class="shell story-grid">
          <div class="story-copy" data-reveal>
            <h2>Round-trip, with receipts.</h2>
            <p class="lead">
              The repo ships a small authored demo project. Compile it on VFP9, decompile it with the CLI, recompile the output: statement frames and symbol tables come back byte-identical. Exit codes, hashes, and per-section verdicts are committed alongside it.
            </p>
            <p>
              Every benchmark corpus is public VFP code on GitHub. On the development corpus — <a href="https://github.com/luhongbin/YiFeiERP">luhongbin/YiFeiERP</a>, one vendor's real ERP estate of forms and class libraries — 10,514 of 10,514 method sections lift, none blocked, none silently dropped. On repositories never used to infer schemas — <a href="https://github.com/VFPX/AATest">VFPX/AATest</a>, <a href="https://github.com/RubenAlejandroFalcon/ARoMal">ARoMal</a>, and <a href="https://github.com/VFPX/ReportingApps">VFPX/ReportingApps</a> for validation, plus held-out repos such as <a href="https://github.com/ankuryu/vfpObjbill">vfpObjbill</a> opened once per round and never tuned on — every validation section lifts (2,007 of 2,007) and 95.7% of held-out sections lift. Recompiling the decompiled output on a real VFP9 reproduces the original bytecode frames for 29,579 of 29,597 development form and class sections; every remaining difference is a documented, oracle-proven loss in compilation, never a guess.
            </p>
            <p>
              Some things provably cannot come back: comments are never compiled, DECLARE parameter names are discarded, WAIT WINDOW clause order is canonicalised by the compiler, and some identifiers are blanked by the compiler itself. FoxLift proves what is unrecoverable and never invents it.
            </p>
            <a class="button" href="https://github.com/foxlift/foxlift/tree/main/demo">See the demo receipts</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section story" id="status">
        <div class="shell story-grid">
          <div class="story-copy" data-reveal>
            <h2>Where we stand.</h2>
            <p class="lead">
              Method code comes back today; whole rebuildable projects are the roadmap. In between, partial results are the contract, not a failure mode.
            </p>

            <ol class="timeline" aria-label="Project status timeline">
              <li>
                <p class="timeline-date">Aug 27</p>
                <div class="timeline-body">
                  <h3>v0.1.0 — first public release</h3>
                  <p>
                    Forms and class libraries (.scx/.vcx), compiled programs (.fxp), and module
                    scans of .app/.exe decompile to per-module VFP source with symbols and
                    per-section verdicts. Round-trip demo with committed
                    receipts. On <a href="https://pypi.org/project/foxlift/">PyPI</a> and <a href="https://github.com/foxlift/foxlift">GitHub</a>.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class="timeline-date">Aug 28</p>
                <div class="timeline-body">
                  <h3>v0.2.0 — compiled programs and menus</h3>
                  <p>
                    DEFINE CLASS scaffolds in .fxp, compiled menu programs (.mpx), SQL decode, and
                    non-Latin code pages. Recompiling the decompiled output on real VFP9
                    reproduces the original bytecode frames for 95% of the measured population.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class="timeline-date">Aug 31</p>
                <div class="timeline-body">
                  <h3>v0.3.0 — whole applications, and closed parity</h3>
                  <p>
                    <code>foxlift extract</code> unpacks an .exe or .app into a named project
                    tree — compiled members lifted back to source, tables and resources
                    byte-for-byte — and the library reassembles a .pjx that VFP9 rebuilds
                    headless. Parity on the development corpus is closed: every method section
                    lifts, 29,579 of 29,597 form and class sections and 2,007 of 2,007
                    validation sections recompile frame-equal, and every remaining difference
                    is a documented, oracle-proven loss in compilation.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class="timeline-date">Sep 4</p>
                <div class="timeline-body">
                  <h3>v0.4.0 — a second corpus, and conservation</h3>
                  <p>
                    A second benchmark corpus — 29 public repositories, 170,820 modules —
                    measured under the same rules: 101,719 of 103,363 development
                    sections lift with zero leaked bytes. Every declared member of every
                    module on both corpora now leaves a trace in the lifted source. The
                    held-out repositories, opened once at the wave close, lift 1,015 of
                    1,030 sections. Dozens of statement and expression banks measured whole
                    on the oracle: the SET family, BROWSE and popup menus, the DO family,
                    REPORT FORM, SQL unions and join chains, the scope operator, and the
                    folded LINENO() line layout.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li data-now>
                <p class="timeline-date">Now</p>
                <div class="timeline-body">
                  <h3>Currently working on: the long tail of the second corpus</h3>
                  <p>
                    The same census-and-proof loop, pointed at what still refuses on the
                    second corpus: the SQL SELECT column forms, CREATE CURSOR field banks,
                    the unmeasured statement leads, and the assignment-target spellings —
                    each family measured whole against the real VFP9 oracle before it ships.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class="timeline-date">Next</p>
                <div class="timeline-body">
                  <h3>Reports and the rest</h3>
                  <p>
                    Report definitions (.frx) and the remaining artifact types close out the
                    set, so a whole application round-trips without exceptions.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
            </ol>

            <p>
              Hand FoxLift any binary and it tells the truth about it. A file that is not VFP is refused cleanly. A VFP binary always yields whatever is provable: every module found is decompiled, and any statement beyond the measured slice stays in the output as an annotated placeholder naming the exact blocker — never silently dropped. Exit 0 with a verified verdict means a 100% lift; exit 2 marks a partial.
            </p>
            <p>
              Concretely: a real production .exe from the development corpus currently comes back as 13 modules and roughly 10,900 lines of source — 159 of 160 sections lifted, and the one miss is labeled in place with the unmeasured SET variant that blocks it.
            </p>
            <p>
              Scope is Visual FoxPro 9 (SP2). Every claim above is measured against a real VFP9 compiler; nothing is guessed.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

